          "text": "---\n\n**Question 2: Who are the actors?**\n\nThe code uses \"Patient\" everywhere — `patientContext`, system prompts say \"Patient symptoms,\" etc. But this is a demo system with zero real patients. And the admin panel implies a second type of user.\n\nThe system prompt already hedges: *\"You are a medical triage assistant... This is a DEMONSTRATION system. All data is synthetic.\"*\n\nI see three distinct roles that need names:\n\n| Role | What they do | Current terms |\n|------|-------------|---------------|\n| Person using the interview UI | Fills out symptom form, views results | \"patient,\" \"user\" |\n| Person using the admin panel | Views submissions, triggers synthetic cases, checks stats | \"admin\" |\n| The hypothetical person whose symptoms are reported | Exists only in the data — the \"subject\" of the triage | \"patient\" |\n\n**My recommendation:**\n- **User** — the person at the keyboard interacting with the frontend (submitting, viewing results)\n- **Admin** — the authenticated person using the dashboard (reviewing submissions, managing synthetic data)\n- **Patient** — the hypothetical person whose symptoms are being described in a Triage Submission (exists only as data — age, gender, symptoms). A User may report their own symptoms (User = Patient) or someone else's (User = receptionist entering for a Patient).\n\nThe Patient is always synthetic in this demo, but the concept is needed for the domain model.\n\n**Does this three-way split (User / Admin / Patient) work for you? Or should \"User\" and \"Patient\" be collapsed since in most demo scenarios they're the same person?**",
