        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Good. Now the big question about the interview dynamics. Let me re-read the user's response...\n\n\"Questions should be result of processing user initial submission. For example 'my head is going to explode', and AI returns 'its like pulsating pain in one spot you can point or whole head hurts'?\"\n\nSo the flow is:\n1. User enters free-text initial complaint\n2. AI responds with a follow-up question tailored to the symptoms\n3. User answers\n4. AI may ask another follow-up question\n5. Eventually, AI produces the final triage result\n\nThis is essentially a medical conversation/interview driven by AI. This is much more sophisticated than a static questionnaire.\n\nKey architectural implications:\n- The frontend needs a chat/conversation UI (not a form wizard)\n- Each turn is an AI call\n- The AI needs to track conversation state\n- The AI decides when to stop asking and produce a result\n\nQuestions I need to ask:\n- How many rounds max? (To prevent runaway conversations)\n- Does AI decide when to stop, or is there a fixed number?\n- What's the initial input called? \"Initial symptom description\"? \"Chief complaint\"?\n- The entire conversation + result = the Triage Submission. But each individual message — what's that called? A \"turn\"? A \"message\"?\n\nLet me ask about the interview dynamics. I think the key decision is: who controls when the interview ends?",
