        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---\n\n**Question 4: Interview dynamics — who controls the conversation flow?**\n\nThis is the critical architectural decision. The AI-driven interview is two fundamentally different patterns:\n\n| Pattern | How it works | UX |\n|---------|-------------|-----|\n| **A: Fixed rounds** | Always N turns. AI generates N follow-up questions upfront, User answers them sequentially, AI produces result after last answer. | Wizard-like, predictable |\n| **B: AI-driven (chat)** | User enters initial complaint → AI asks one question → User answers → AI decides whether to ask another or produce result. Conversation ends when AI signals \"done.\" | Chat-like, adaptive |\n\nYour example (\"my head is going to explode\") sounds like **Pattern B** — each AI response is either a follow-up question *or* a final result.\n\n**My recommendation: Pattern B with guardrails.**\n- The AI drives the conversation — it asks one question at a time based on what it learns\n- The AI decides when it has enough information to produce a result\n- **Guardrails**: Max 3-5 exchanges (AI turns), enforced by the backend. If the AI hasn't produced a result by the limit, the backend forces it to produce one with the information gathered so far\n- The full transcript (initial complaint + all Q&A + final result) = the Triage Submission\n\nThis gives us the demo \"wow factor\" (adaptive AI interviews) without the risk of infinite conversations or runaway costs.\n\n**A few sub-questions to nail down:**\n\n- **4a.** What do we call the initial free-text input? \"Initial symptom description\"? \"Chief complaint\"?\n- **4b.** What do we call each AI-User exchange? A \"turn\"? A \"message\"?\n- **4c.** Should the AI see the full conversation history each turn, or just the last message? (Full history = better questions but more tokens)\n- **4d.** Pattern B or something else entirely?",
