        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Good. Now let me think about what's left. I think we've covered most of the domain. Let me take stock:\n\nDomain language resolved:\n- Triage Submission (single entity)\n- User (self-registering, also acts as patient)\n- Admin (view-only for submissions, manages users, login-as)\n- Initial Symptom Description\n- Turn (User message + AI response, up to 3)\n- Conversation History (with char limits)\n- Synthetic Case (AI-generated initial description, flows through pipeline)\n\nOther decisions:\n- AI-driven interview, 3 turns max, guarded by backend\n- OpenRouter free models for app, DeepSeek for development\n- Specialist types: 18 (covers major medical domains)\n- Urgency levels: LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, EMERGENCY\n- Character limits: 500/300/1000\n- Error handling: retry up to 3x, transparency\n- User sees result card + submission history\n- Auth: open registration, single JWT with roles\n- Admin: stats row + live feed + specialist/urgency distribution\n\nLet me think about whether there are any ADR-worthy decisions:\n\n1. AI provider choice (OpenRouter free vs DeepSeek) — this was decided for cost reasons. Is it hard to reverse? Medium. Surprising without context? Yes. Result of a real trade-off? Yes (free vs quality). This could be an ADR.\n\n2. Single entity (TriageSubmission) vs split (Submission + Result) — decided. Hard to reverse? Medium. Surprising? Maybe. Trade-off? Simplicity vs normalization. I think this isn't ADR-worthy though — it's a straightforward simplification for a demo.\n\n3. AI-driven conversation vs static form — decided. This IS surprising and non-obvious. Hard to reverse? Yes — changes the entire frontend and backend architecture. Trade-off? Dynamic UX vs simplicity. This IS ADR-worthy.\n\nLet me propose ADRs for these two decisions. But first, let me ask about one more thing: the frontend-backend communication pattern for the interview. The interview is conversational — does the frontend use polling to check for AI responses? This affects the frontend architecture significantly.",
          "time": {
