        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Good. I think we've covered all the major domain/architecture decisions. Let me now assess whether we need any ADRs, and then wrap up the domain language.\n\nADR-worthy decisions so far:\n1. AI Provider: OpenRouter free models for app runtime, DeepSeek for development. This meets all three criteria:\n   - Hard to reverse: Changing provider means rewriting integration\n   - Surprising: The agents.md says DeepSeek throughout, but we're using OpenRouter\n   - Real trade-off: Cost (free) vs. quality/performance, different API\n\n2. DDD Light architecture with bounded contexts - this is already documented in agents.md. But is it ADR-worthy?\n   - Hard to reverse: Yes, it's the architectural foundation\n   - Surprising: Some might expect \"standard\" Symfony flat structure\n   - Real trade-off: Structure vs. simplicity for a 2-week project\n   - But... it's already fully documented in agents.md. The agents.md IS the record. An ADR might be redundant.\n\n3. Single entity (no separate TriageResult) - this is a domain decision, not really hard to reverse. Not ADR-worthy.\n\nI think the AI Provider switch is the one clear ADR candidate. Let me also check: the interview pattern (AI-driven, 3 turns max, full history, polling) — is that ADR-worthy?\n\n- Hard to reverse: Medium. Changing from conversational to static form would touch frontend + backend + AI prompting.\n- Surprising: Maybe. A standard medical app would use static forms, not AI-driven conversation.\n- Real trade-off: Dynamic/adaptive vs. predictable/controlled.\n\nHmm, it's borderline. But I think it's more of a product decision than an architecture decision. Skip it.\n\nLet me offer the AI Provider ADR and then check for any remaining domain language gaps.",
